A||||6||Fiero*101||Why do I have such acceleration?||||1||
Z||000000||Fiero*101||10-14-2001||01:22 AM||||Im sure i spelled somthign wrong up there <BR>but My 86 SE 2M6 Auto does not get up and go. It takes awile for it to get up to speed from a dead stop. Why is this?<BR>is it b/c its an old engine and has 152,000 miles on it or could it be b/c i dont have an exhaust sytem on it? I was told b/c its a Fiero and they dont have good pick up.<BR>But i know thats a bunch of BS So any help would be great.<BR>oh and by the way it just got a tune up all new plugs and wires and distributor.<P><P>------------------<BR>Fiero*101<BR>Gold 86 SE 2M6||||reg||1
Z||000001||SLYGUY||10-14-2001||01:44 AM||||My 88GT 5spd positively RIPS!!<BR>Very strong acceleration. <BR>Haven't seen the top speed yet. I figure between 125-145mph though.<P>That automatic is the reason why you have very little acceleration. Try manually shifting it. Don't allow a shift under four thousand rpms.<BR>That should help. <BR>Fieros don't just look exotic, they drive that way too!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Sly<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/SLYGUYSIG.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Fiero*101||10-14-2001||01:59 AM||||Thanks Sly<BR>but i do that <BR>When i say poor accel i mean POOR Accel my moms mini van gets better pick up then that hell my sisters Buick Somerset has better pick up then my 86 SE<P>So does any body know why it does this?<P>------------------<BR>Fiero*101<BR>Gold 86 SE 2M6||||reg||1
Z||000003||Rick 88||10-14-2001||02:08 AM||||Maybe your torque convertor is locked up. This could sap a lot of off the line acceleration. I don't have an automatic but some of the guys can tell you how to check this.<P>Rick 88||||reg||1
Z||000004||Standard||10-14-2001||02:12 AM||||Well, having 152,000 miles doesn't help anything.. also, what do you mean by "i don't have an exhaust system on it"?  Taking off the cat and muffler will cause you to lose torque, which wouldn't be too big of a deal if you had a manual, but with an automatic.. the more torque the better.  <P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_pic_full.jpg">         <BR>1988 Quad 4 Coupe 5 speed<BR><FONT SIZE="1">1986 GT 5 speed, 1984 SC Auto<BR><FONT COLOR="red">AOL: LastMinuteBastrd  AIM: SavedbyJebus</FONT></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Smoooooth GT||10-14-2001||12:01 PM||||when I bought an aftermarkrt dist. I installed it and set the timing by ear...  I had it off some and had noooo get up at all.. I would push the gas and it was like a go-cart.. seemed like it had to wind up or something..  I did some adjusting on it and now will scream both tires on dry pavement, and I have a auto also..  try this, you never know, just m,ight help some...  Steve<P>------------------<BR>NOS Injected 2.8L V/6<BR>UNDER CONSTRUCTION   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/smooth_sig2_4_1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||California Kid||10-14-2001||12:31 PM||||Along with the other comments above, it is known that the timing degree stamping, which is held on by fasteners, can come loose. This will result in a incorrect timing setting that severly affects performance. Check it to see if you can move it by hand, if it's loose, that's your problem. A real good mechanic will know how to determine TDC (Top Dead Center), re-position degree indicator, and torque it down.||||reg||1
